<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 10px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Can Count! – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Hold Up Ten Fingers!</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Can Count!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: reading_1: Recognize basic familiar words supported with illustrations or visual aids. reading_2: Show comprehension of storybook sequencing by pointing to pictures with the teacher's guidance. phonemic_awareness: Recognize familiar letters.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can point to the correct number of objects when asked and supported with a picture (e.g., Teacher says, 'How many pencils?' and student points to five pencils in a book). Can sequence storybook pictures involving numbers (e.g., placing images of one, two, and three apples in order). Can recognize the letters 'F, G, H, I, and J' in words when supported by corresponding pictures (e.g., 'F' in 'fish').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Count Your Fingers" song, asking students to hold up their fingers and count 1-10 aloud. Teacher uses large flashcards for numbers 1-10 and basic objects (e.g., one red block, two yellow pencils). Students point to the corresponding number of fingers as they count. This activates prior vocabulary (numbers, hand, finger) and introduces the initial /k/ in 'count' and /f/ in 'fingers' sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard showing a picture of three blue blocks and the number '3' written below it. The teacher says, "Look! Three blocks. Can you see the number three? This is the word 'three'." The teacher traces the word 'three' with a finger. The teacher then points to a flashcard with 'F' and a 'fish' and says, "This is the letter F. F for fish." and traces the letter. This models visual recognition of numbers, words, and letters, crucial for reading.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a flashcard with '5' and five pencils) "Is this number seven?" (Expected answer: "No!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of four crayons) "How many crayons?" (Expected answer: "Four!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Hold Up Ten Fingers!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, simple illustrated storybook or big picture cards with very short sentences about counting school supplies (e.g., "One red book.", "Two yellow pencils.", "Three blue blocks.", "Four green crayons.", "Five orange markers."). The text will be simple sentences with quantity + nouns and adjectives. The story will also subtly include words starting with letters F, G, H, I, J (e.g., a 'fish' sticker on a page, 'juice' box in a picture).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will recognize basic familiar words supported with illustrations or visual aids while listening to a story about counting objects.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the story slowly, pointing to the words and the corresponding pictures. Students listen and follow along visually.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is this story about?" (Expected: "Numbers!" or "Counting!"). "What things can you see?" (Expected: "Pencils!", "Books!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching (Teacher-guided):</b> "Point to the two yellow pencils." "Show me the three blue blocks." (Students point in the book/on the cards).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Letter spotting:</b> "Can you see the letter 'F' in this picture (pointing to 'fish')?" Students try to spot and point to the letter.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice recognizing number words (1-10) and basic object words (book, pencil, block, crayon, marker, glue, ink, juice) supported by visuals. They will also practice recognizing letters F, G, H, I, J in familiar words. Grammar: Quantity + nouns; Wh-questions ('How many?'); Yes/No questions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Matching Numbers and Words (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher provides students with a worksheet. On one side, there are numerals (1-5) and on the other, corresponding pictures of objects (e.g., one book, two pencils, three blocks, four crayons, five markers) with their written number words (one, two, three, four, five) below them. Students draw lines to match the numeral to the correct picture and number word. Teacher prompts: "Match number one to 'one book'."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Letter Hunt (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher displays large flashcards with pictures of objects or animals whose names start with F, G, H, I, J (e.g., Fish, Glue, Hand, Ink, Juice) with the word written clearly. The teacher asks, "Can you find the letter 'F' in 'fish'?" Students point to the letter 'F'. The teacher then asks, "What letter is this?" pointing to 'G' in 'glue'. Students identify the letter and the word. This reinforces phonemic awareness by recognizing letter sounds (F, G, H, I, J) in words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. "How Many?" Point and Read (Guided Practice):</b> Students work with their worksheets or a small picture book. The teacher says, "Show me two pencils!" Students point to the picture of two pencils. The teacher then asks, "How many pencils?" and students are encouraged to say "Two!" while pointing. The teacher then asks, "Is this number four?" pointing to a picture with four blocks. Students respond "Yes!" or "No!" This practices answering quantity questions and recognizing numbers visually.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Hold Up Ten Fingers!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Picture Sequencing and 'Reading' activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity: "My Counting Book"</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives each student a set of simple, cut-out pictures representing a sequential count (e.g., a picture of one apple, two oranges, three bananas, four grapes, five strawberries). Each picture also has the numeral and the corresponding word (e.g., '1' and 'one apple').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are instructed to sequence these pictures in order from 1 to 5 to create their own "Counting Book" by gluing them onto blank pages or a long strip of paper.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Once assembled, students "read" their books in small groups or with the teacher. They point to each picture and the number/word, saying, "One apple," "Two oranges," etc. The teacher encourages counting aloud and asking peers, "How many grapes?" or "What number is this?" while pointing. This activity focuses on showing comprehension of storybook sequencing by pointing to pictures and recognizing familiar words/numbers with visual aids. Turn-taking and active listening are encouraged.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> During the "My Counting Book" activity, the teacher circulates and observes students. The teacher asks individual students to "Show me three blocks" in their book or a flashcard, and "Point to the number six." The teacher also asks, "Can you find the letter 'J'?" on a card with 'juice'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to point to the correct number of objects (1-10) when asked.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to sequence storybook pictures involving numbers in correct order.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to recognize and point to letters 'F, G, H, I, J' in words/pictures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempt to answer "How many?" questions (even with pointing or one-word answers).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> The teacher gives each student a small paper with three pictures: one showing '7 pencils', another showing the letter 'I' in 'ink', and a third showing '4 blocks'. Students circle the number of pencils, point to the letter 'I', and hold up four fingers for the blocks. The teacher asks: "Did you find the number?" and "Did you find the letter?"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite picture in our counting book today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you show me a number you know how to read now?" (Students point to a number word/numeral).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students were able to recognize and point to numbers 1-5 with support, and sequencing was generally successful. Some students still confuse 'G' and 'J' or need more practice matching number words to numerals. The "My Counting Book" was highly engaging and promoted good turn-taking. For the next lesson, more individualized support for letter recognition will be beneficial, and I will prepare more visual aids for numbers 6-10. I need to ensure the quantity + noun structure is reinforced more orally.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on reading numbers and words with pictures. The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction) will build on this by having students orally describe quantities of objects they see and ask "How many?" questions to their peers, using the recognized vocabulary in interactive speaking tasks.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 minutes</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>